## What I did

`src/router.ts`: splits pattern and path on `/`, filters empty segments (leading/trailing slashes irrelevant, interior `//` collapses), validates the pattern before looking at the path (`*` only allowed as the last segment → `TypeError`), cuts the path's query at the first `?`, compares literals undecoded/case-sensitive, captures `:name` via `decodeURIComponent`, and a trailing `*` records `params.rest` = remaining segments each decoded then joined with `/` (`""` when zero). Segment-count checks per spec, `{}` on full match. Also pulled the capture-or-compare step into a small local closure so both the no-star and star branches share it.

`src/router.test.ts`: all the listed cases plus a few I judged safe (decode inside rest, both directions of root mismatch, `*`+query).

## Behavior call the spec didn't pin down

Validation runs on the **empty-dropped** segments, not the raw `/` split. So a `*` followed only by trailing slashes — e.g. `match("/static/*/", "/static/a/b")` → `{ rest: "a/b" }` — is accepted, because rule 3 says trailing slashes are irrelevant "on both sides"; validating the raw split would make the trailing slash after `*` invalidate an otherwise-fine pattern. Every listed case (`/a/*/b`, `/*/x`, even with a non-matching path) still throws `TypeError` either way; only trailing-`/`-after-`*` patterns differ. If your reference implementation validates the raw split instead, that single case (`"/static/*/"` style) is the only divergence — say the word and I'll switch it.
